research.designSpec:
CLIP ENCODING + MUXING PIPELINE - IMPLEMENTATION SPEC (Hank's Hits, 2026-09-27)
Scope: everything from the moment a compositor frame or a game-audio sample exists, up to a finished `File('...mp4', {type:'video/mp4'})`, a crash-safe long recording in OPFS, and editor re-renders. The capture sources (per-game hooks), compositor painting, audio-bus migration, share UI and hosting belong to other tracks. This spec fixes the contracts those tracks must meet.

=== 0. WHAT WAS PROVEN TODAY (prototype in /tmp/hh-clips/proto, prod-identical CSP, M5 Mac) ===
- A single Output built "last 30 s" from a ring of 1 s-GOP H.264 packets plus AAC packets: 12.8 ms (Chrome 153) and 19 ms (Safari 26.5).
- Frame-accurate start trim with no re-encode, via negative timestamps and a mediabunny elst. ffprobe shows 29.500 s. Flash frames and beeps align to 0.02 ms after AAC priming compensation.
- Title card spliced by packet copy. A second VideoEncoder with the identical config produced byte-identical avcC in both browsers, and ffmpeg decoded the result cleanly.
- WebKit 302253 reproduced (raw esds bytes). mediabunny AudioSampleSource emits the fixed ASC '1190'.
- VideoFrame transfer to a worker works in Safari 26.5 and Chrome 153.
- SharedArrayBuffer is undefined in both browsers.
- MediaRecorder with timeslice 1000 gives one keyframe-led fragment per second (Safari by default; Chrome with videoKeyFrameIntervalDuration). mediabunny remuxes a fragment ring into an exact-length fast-start MP4.
- Safari OPFS sync-handle writes honor view offsets.
- A fragmented recording truncated at a committed moof recovered and was finalized into a fast-start MP4 (moov at byte 32).
Evidence files: /tmp/hh-clips/proto/out/*.json, *.mp4. Scripts: /tmp/hh-clips/proto/*-src.js and /tmp/hh-clips/bundle/{remux-test,record-test}.mjs.
NOT proven: any phone. Section 10 lists the mandatory device matrix.

=== 1. THREADS, MODULES, FILES (all machinery in apps/web/src/shared/clips; games only call registration hooks) ===
Main thread (per game page, loaded lazily after GameShell mounts on a capable device):
- runtime/capabilities.ts: tier probe, cached per UA+preset in localStorage key `hh.clips.caps.v1`.
- runtime/framePump.ts: owns the compositor canvas handle, grid pacing, drop gates and the Timeline source of truth.
- runtime/audioTap.ts: attaches the tap AudioWorkletNode to any AudioContext, including same-origin iframe realms. Also runs ClockSync.
- runtime/mediaRecorderTier.ts: tier T2 only.
- runtime/engineClient.ts: typed RPC to the worker.
- ClipProvider.tsx: React context in GameShell. Uses the useSyncExternalStore mounted pattern to avoid hydration #418.
Audio render thread: public/clips/tap-worklet.v1.js, the hh-clip-tap processor. Plain JS with no imports; a same-origin URL is required because script-src has no blob:.
Engine worker, one per game page, created at arm time and terminated on unmount (engine/engine.worker.ts, module worker):
- liveVideo.ts: VideoEncoder plus the T3 client.
- videoRing.ts.
- timeline.ts: mirror of the main-thread timeline.
- audioIngest.ts: clock map, timeline map, resample, mix, jitter buffer.
- pcmRing.ts.
- aacHarness.ts: mediabunny AudioSampleSource on a NullTarget feeding an AAC ring.
- priming.ts.
- assemble.ts.
- poster.ts.
- record.ts and opfs.ts.
- fmp4Segmenter.ts: T2 fragment parsing and remux.
- editor.ts: lazy import containing smart-cut, cards, render pass and share-size re-encode.
- protocol.ts.
codecs/aac-wasm/: our own FFmpeg aacenc WASM loader plus a CustomAudioEncoder. It runs in-process in the engine worker.
codecs/h264-wasm/: minih264 WASM encoder. Tier T3 only, in its own worker.
public/clips/wasm/: aac.simd.<sha8>.wasm, aac.scalar.<sha8>.wasm, h264.simd.<sha8>.wasm, h264.scalar.<sha8>.wasm (committed artifacts with a SHA256SUMS file).
public/licenses/: FFmpeg source tarball, build script, LGPL text.

=== 2. CAPABILITY PROBE AND TIER SELECTION (feature detection only, never UA sniffing except the documented priming constants) ===
T1 WebCodecs: VideoEncoder H.264 probe-encode passes. Audio is native AAC if canEncodeAudio('aac', {numberOfChannels:2, sampleRate:48000, quality:new Quality(128000)}); otherwise our WASM AAC, the SIMD build if WebAssembly.validate(simdProbe) and the scalar build if not.
T2 MediaRecorder: no working VideoEncoder, but HTMLCanvasElement.captureStream exists and MediaRecorder.isTypeSupported('video/mp4') is true. That profile describes iOS/iPadOS 15.4-16.3 and macOS Safari 14.1-16.3.
T3 WASM H.264: no avc encoder at all, but WebAssembly and OffscreenCanvas exist (Firefox Android, Chrome Android < 150 without HW H.264, Firefox desktop < 130).
T3-lite: T3 without WASM SIMD or without working captureStream (iOS 14.3-15.3). Scalar WASM at 480x270, 15-20 fps. Audio comes from a MediaRecorder('audio/mp4') fragment ring fed by a MediaStreamAudioDestinationNode.
NONE: no WebAssembly and no encoder (for example Lockdown Mode on a pre-26 iOS). The brag-card path runs, and the UI says why.
Probe order, run in the engine worker at first arm and cached:
```ts
const PRESET = { landscape:[1280,720], portrait:[720,1280], '4:3':[960,720], '3:4':[720,960], square:[720,720], hdLandscape:[1920,1088/*crop 1080 via fit*/]} as const; // all multiples of 16
function avcCandidates(w:number,h:number){ const lvl = (Math.ceil(w/16)*Math.ceil(h/16) > 3600) ? '28' : '1f'; // L3.1 MaxFS 3600 MBs = 720p30 exactly; L4.0 for 1080p30
  return [`avc1.6400${lvl}`, `avc1.4d40${lvl}`, `avc1.4200${lvl}`]; }
async function pickVideoConfig(w:number,h:number,fps:number,bitrate:number){
  for (const codec of avcCandidates(w,h)) for (const bitrateMode of ['variable','constant'] as const) {
    const cfg: VideoEncoderConfig = { codec, width:w, height:h, bitrate, bitrateMode, framerate:fps,
      latencyMode:'realtime',            // no frame reordering, so decode order = presentation order; exact end-trims
      hardwareAcceleration:'no-preference', // prefer-hardware rejects SW-only devices; prefer-software forces OpenH264
      avc:{ format:'avc' } };           // avcC description for MP4 (annexb would need avc3)
    try { if (!(await VideoEncoder.isConfigSupported(cfg)).supported) continue; } catch { continue; } // iOS can THROW (mediabunny #456)
    if (await probeEncode(cfg, 2000)) return cfg; // isConfigSupported is unreliable on Firefox (mediabunny #222) and on some MediaCodec builds
  }
  return null;
}
async function probeEncode(cfg:VideoEncoderConfig, timeoutMs:number){ // key + delta frame; success = output with decoderConfig.description
  return new Promise<boolean>(res => { let ok=false; const t=setTimeout(()=>{try{e.close()}catch{} res(false)},timeoutMs);
    const e = new VideoEncoder({ output:(c,m)=>{ if(m?.decoderConfig?.description) ok=true; }, error:()=>{clearTimeout(t);res(false)} });
    e.configure(cfg); const px=new Uint8Array(cfg.width*cfg.height*4);
    for (let i=0;i<2;i++){ const f=new VideoFrame(px,{format:'RGBA',codedWidth:cfg.width,codedHeight:cfg.height,timestamp:i*33333}); e.encode(f,{keyFrame:i===0}); f.close(); }
    e.flush().then(()=>{clearTimeout(t); e.close(); res(ok);}, ()=>{clearTimeout(t); res(false);}); });
}
```
Bitrates at 30 fps VBR: 3D (monster-truck, four-wheeler-3d) 3.0 Mbps. 2D arcade 2.0 Mbps. Retro and pixel art (nearest-neighbor integer upscale) 2.0 Mbps. DOM-replay boards 1.0 Mbps (offline encode). The HD preset (desktop or Chromebook opt-in, only when the compositor cost stays under budget) runs at 6.0 Mbps. Audio is AAC-LC, 48 kHz stereo, 128 kbps (the Windows MF encoder accepts only 96/128/160/192). File size is about 11.6 MB for 30 s at 3.1 Mbps and about 7.9 MB at 2.1 Mbps.

=== 3. VIDEO PATH (T1) ===
3.1 Fixed output per session. The compositor paints the game frame, HUD strip and branding into ONE fixed preset for the whole session, letterboxing or pillarboxing on rotation. Dimensions are multiples of 16. That satisfies Safari/VideoToolbox's even-dimension rule (mediabunny throws on odd dimensions) and avoids the padded-coded-width green-edge report in mediabunny #302. The encoder is therefore never reconfigured for rotation.
3.2 Frame pump contract. Sources call `pump.tick(paint, now)` synchronously, in the same task that rendered the game:
- R3F: addAfterEffect, never useFrame.
- 2D games: at the end of the game's own rAF draw.
- iframe games: our own rAF wrapper inside the iframe realm, installed before the game script runs (in public/emulator/index.html and the four-wheeler srcdoc). A parent rAF runs BEFORE the child's in the same rendering update, so it would read a cleared WebGL buffer.
The compositor is a main-thread OffscreenCanvas (T2 needs an HTMLCanvasElement, see 7). Context options: getContext('2d', {alpha:false}).
```ts
class FramePump {
  fps = 30; private next = 0; private inFlight = 0; private ewma = 0; private lastTs = -1; dropped = 0;
  constructor(private w: Worker, private canvas: OffscreenCanvas, private ctx: OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D, private tl: Timeline){}
  tick(paint:(c:OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D)=>void, now = performance.now()){
    if (!this.tl.isLive || now + 2 < this.next) return;                 // 2 ms tolerance; stay on the grid, no catch-up bursts
    const period = 1000/this.fps; this.next = Math.max(this.next + period, now - period);
    if (this.inFlight >= 2) { this.dropped++; return; }                  // backpressure = drop, never wait
    const tlUs = this.tl.toTimelineUs(now); const ts = Math.round(tlUs/ (1e6/this.fps)) * Math.round(1e6/this.fps);
    if (ts <= this.lastTs) return; this.lastTs = ts;
    const t0 = performance.now(); paint(this.ctx);
    const frame = new VideoFrame(this.canvas, { timestamp: ts, duration: Math.round(1e6/this.fps), alpha:'discard' });
    this.w.postMessage({ t:'frame', frame }, [frame]); this.inFlight++;   // worker replies {t:'consumed'} -> inFlight--
    this.ewma = this.ewma*0.9 + (performance.now()-t0)*0.1; this.adapt();  // budget 3 ms @60 Hz, 6 ms @30 Hz: 30->24->20->15 fps; step back up after 10 s under budget
  }
}
```
Fallback when postMessage transfer throws DataCloneError: run VideoEncoder on the main thread (encode() is async and cheap) and post the copied packets to the worker.
3.3 Worker encode loop:
```ts
onFrame(f: VideoFrame){
  if (enc.state!=='configured' || enc.encodeQueueSize >= 2) { f.close(); stats.drop++; post({t:'consumed'}); return; }
  const key = forceKey || f.timestamp - lastKeyUs >= 1_000_000;       // 1 s GOP = replay start granularity; verify chunk.type, not the request
  if (key) { lastKeyUs = f.timestamp; forceKey = false; }
  enc.encode(f, { keyFrame: key }); f.close(); post({t:'consumed'});
}
enc = new VideoEncoder({ output: onChunk, error: onEncErr });
function onChunk(chunk: EncodedVideoChunk, meta?: EncodedVideoChunkMetadata){
  if (meta?.decoderConfig) maybeNewEpoch(meta.decoderConfig);          // compare description BYTES; copy them (they may be views)
  const data = new Uint8Array(chunk.byteLength); chunk.copyTo(data);
  let type = chunk.type; if (epochPacketCount===0 && type==='delta' && hasIdrNal(data)) type='key'; // mediabunny #365 guard
  videoRing.push({ type, ts: chunk.timestamp, dur: chunk.duration ?? frameDurUs, data, epoch: epoch.id });
  recorder?.onVideoPacket(...);                                        // record mode tees the SAME packets: no second encoder
}
function onEncErr(e: DOMException){ // QuotaExceededError = reclaimed (background + pressure + 90 s idle, reclaimable_codec.h); others = platform failure
  epochClosed(); enc = recreate(sameConfig); forceKey = true; report('encoder-restarted', e.name); }
```
Keyframes are also forced on the first frame after resume and after any drop streak longer than 500 ms. Scene-change keyframes are allowed, so GOPs vary in length.
3.4 Why the compositor is NOT transferControlToOffscreen: WebGL without preserveDrawingBuffer must be read in the same task that rendered it, on the main thread. Moving the compositor into the worker would need a per-frame ImageBitmap hand-off, which adds cost and gains nothing: measured main-thread VideoFrame cost is about 0.1 ms in Chrome and about 1-2 ms in Safari. The worker owns everything heavy: encoding, rings, muxing, OPFS and WASM.
3.5 Reconfiguration policy. Never reconfigure the running encoder mid-session. A preset change (for example the kid switching clip orientation) flushes, closes and creates a new encoder, which starts epoch N+1 with a forced keyframe. A clip that spans epochs with byte-identical avcC and equal dimensions is seamless. Otherwise the editor render pass re-encodes the older portion. There is never a silent truncation; the UI states the real length.
3.6 VideoRing and memory budget:
```ts
interface Pkt { type:'key'|'delta'; ts:number; dur:number; data:Uint8Array; epoch:number }
interface Gop { epoch:number; start:number; end:number; bytes:number; pkts:Pkt[] }
push(p){ if (p.type==='key' || !cur || cur.epoch!==p.epoch) { cur={epoch:p.epoch,start:p.ts,end:p.ts,bytes:0,pkts:[]}; gops.push(cur); }
  cur.pkts.push(p); cur.bytes+=p.data.byteLength; cur.end=p.ts+p.dur; bytes+=p.data.byteLength;
  // keep full coverage: drop the oldest GOP only while the SECOND-oldest still starts >= maxUs back
  while (gops.length>1 && (cur.end - gops[1].start >= maxUs || bytes > budget)) bytes -= gops.shift()!.bytes;
  availableSec = (cur.end - gops[0].start)/1e6; }   // shown to the UI when a budget trim cut coverage (never silent)
```
Defaults: maxUs is 60 s, and budget is 60 s x bitrate/8 x 2 (about 45 MB at 3 Mbps), which is headroom for keyframe-heavy content. On devices with navigator.deviceMemory <= 2, or after any allocation or encoder-quota failure, drop to 30 s and say so in the UI. The epoch table keeps {codec, codedWidth, codedHeight, description bytes, colorSpace, encoderConfig} for every epoch still referenced by a GOP.

=== 4. AUDIO PATH ===
4.1 Tap processor (public/clips/tap-worklet.v1.js):
```js
class HhClipTap extends AudioWorkletProcessor {
  constructor(o){ super(); this.N=o.processorOptions.batchFrames; this.buf=new Int16Array(this.N*2); this.n=0; this.f0=-1; this.sink=this.port; this.on=false;
    this.port.onmessage=e=>{ if(e.data.t==='sink') this.sink=e.data.port; else if(e.data.t==='on') this.on=e.data.on; }; }
  process(inputs){ if(!this.on) return true; const ch=inputs[0]||[]; const L=ch[0], R=ch[1]||ch[0]; const len=L?L.length:128;
    for(let i=0;i<len;i++){ if(this.f0<0) this.f0=currentFrame+i; const l=L?L[i]:0, r=R?R[i]:0;
      this.buf[2*this.n]=l<=-1?-32768:l>=1?32767:(l*32767)|0; this.buf[2*this.n+1]=r<=-1?-32768:r>=1?32767:(r*32767)|0;
      if(++this.n===this.N){ this.sink.postMessage({t:'pcm',f0:this.f0,sr:sampleRate,pcm:this.buf.buffer},[this.buf.buffer]); this.buf=new Int16Array(this.N*2); this.n=0; this.f0=-1; } }
    return true; } }
registerProcessor('hh-clip-tap', HhClipTap);
```
Wiring: `bus -> tap(AudioWorkletNode, numberOfInputs 1, numberOfOutputs 1, outputChannelCount [2], channelCountMode 'explicit') -> GainNode(gain 0) -> destination`. The zero-gain sink guarantees the node is pulled on every engine. batchFrames is 2048 (42.7 ms at 48 kHz, about 23 messages/s, 8 KB each, zero-copy). Transport without SharedArrayBuffer (verified unavailable): a MessageChannel port goes to the worklet and its twin to the engine worker (`tap.port.postMessage({t:'sink',port:p1},[p1])`), and the worklet posts transferable ArrayBuffers. If port transfer into the worklet fails, it falls back to routing through the main thread. One tap exists per AudioContext, including iframe realms (`iframeWin.AudioContext` contexts call `audioWorklet.addModule('/clips/tap-worklet.v1.js')`; the /emulator CSP allows 'self').
4.2 Clock mapping. The main thread samples `(performance.now(), ctx.currentTime, ctx.state)` in the same task every 250 ms while live, and on every 'statechange'. The worker keeps a per-stream offset = median(perfMs - ctxSec*1000) over 8 s, and re-anchors immediately on a state change or a jump of more than 50 ms (suspend, iOS 'interrupted'). Do NOT use getOutputTimestamp: it includes output latency (Bluetooth adds 150-250 ms) that the recording must not inherit. The error bound is about half a render-callback period (plus or minus 5-10 ms on Android). Crystal drift (~50 ppm, about 3 ms/min) is absorbed by the rolling median.
4.3 Timeline, which removes pauses. The main thread is the source of truth: live and paused segments driven by game pause, GameShell pause, document.hidden and 'capture off'. The worker mirrors the segments. Audio passes through a 250 ms jitter buffer so timeline messages always land before the audio they govern. Batches crossing a segment edge are split at the sample; samples inside a paused gap are dropped. Video frames arrive already stamped on the timeline.
4.4 Resample and mix. The ring rate is fixed at 48000 Hz. A tap at another rate (for example a 44.1 kHz iframe context) goes through a 4-point Hermite resampler in the worker. mediabunny's AudioEncoderWrapper throws on any sample-rate change, even with a transform, so we must resample ourselves. Multiple live taps are summed with clamping. The norm is one tap per page (the audio-bus track).
4.5 Two rings:
(a) PcmRing: Int16 interleaved stereo at 11.5 MB/min, 60 s + 2 s. It is the source for the editor and for perfect audio junctions and jingles.
(b) AacRing: continuous AAC-LC packets at about 1 MB/min, produced by a mediabunny AudioSampleSource on a NullTarget Output. This makes 'Clip it!' a pure mux, because clip-time AAC encoding measured 100-160 ms natively and 560 ms for the polyfill on an M5, and will be slower on phones.
```ts
if (!(await canEncodeAudio('aac',{numberOfChannels:2,sampleRate:48000,quality:new Quality(128000)}))) registerOurAacWasm(); // BEFORE creating sources: custom encoders win over native (media-source.ts:2196)
const harness = new Output({ format:new Mp4OutputFormat({ fastStart:'fragmented', minimumFragmentDuration:1 }), target:new NullTarget() }); // fragmented: muxer keeps no per-sample list, so memory stays bounded
const aac = new AudioSampleSource({ codec:'aac', quality:new Quality(128000),
  onEncodedPacket:(p,meta)=>{ if(meta?.decoderConfig) aacCfg = {...meta.decoderConfig, description: copy(meta.decoderConfig.description)}; aacRing.push(p); } }); // meta is ALREADY 302253-fixed (media-source.ts:2259-2296)
harness.addAudioTrack(aac); await harness.start();
// feed on the timeline, shifted by the encoder's priming P so that packet ts t == audio content at t (mediabunny #444)
await aac.add(new AudioSample({ data:int16, format:'s16', numberOfChannels:2, sampleRate:48000, timestamp: tlSec - P/48000 }));
```
Priming P (priming.ts):
- Native encoder: calibrate once per session. Encode 0.5 s of silence with a 1 kHz burst at frame 12000, decode it back with AudioDecoder (fixing the description with ascFor(sr,ch) when it is bad), and P = onset - 12000. Measured 2114 on Chrome/macOS and Safari/macOS; Windows and Android are unknown, which is why calibration is required.
- Our FFmpeg WASM: P = 1024 (measured residual 1090 = 2114 - 1024).
- MediaRecorder (T2/T3-lite on WebKit): 2112 (AudioToolbox), applied at remux.
- Chromium MediaRecorder: 0 (unknown; the error stays under 44 ms, which is below the audio-late detection threshold).
4.6 Our WASM AAC encoder (replaces @mediabunny/aac-encoder):
- Build FFmpeg from a pinned release tarball with mediabunny's documented flags (--disable-everything --enable-encoder=aac, no --enable-gpl) in SIMD (-msimd128) and scalar variants, loaded as separate .wasm files (not inlined).
- Run it in-process in the engine worker: no nested worker and no per-frame postMessage.
- Wrap it as a mediabunny CustomAudioEncoder via registerEncoder, adapting encoder.ts from @mediabunny/aac-encoder (MPL-2.0, headers kept; the repo is public).
- Emit FFmpeg extradata as the description.
- instantiateStreaming(fetch(url)), falling back to arrayBuffer() if the MIME type is wrong. The CSP already has 'unsafe-eval' for WASM compile.

=== 5. CLIP ASSEMBLY ('Clip it!' and auto-highlights), in the worker ===
```ts
async function buildClip({ seconds, endUs = videoRing.endUs, posterAtUs }: ClipReq){
  const zero = endUs - seconds*1e6;                                    // exact requested start
  const gops = videoRing.select(zero, endUs);                          // starts at the last keyframe <= zero
  assertSingleAvcC(gops) || return editor.renderAcrossEpochs(...);     // mixed avcC -> re-encode older part
  const out = new Output({ format:new Mp4OutputFormat({ fastStart:'in-memory' }), target:new BufferTarget() });
  const vs = new EncodedVideoPacketSource('avc'); out.addVideoTrack(vs, { frameRate: fps });
  const as = new EncodedAudioPacketSource('aac'); out.addAudioTrack(as);
  await out.start();
  const v = gops.flatMap(g=>g.pkts).filter(p=>p.ts<endUs).map(p=>({ k:'v' as const, t:(p.ts-zero)/1e6, p })); // pre-roll -> negative -> elst trims it
  const a = aacRing.between(zero/1e6 - 0.0214 /*1 AAC frame pre-roll for MDCT overlap*/, endUs/1e6).map(p=>({ k:'a' as const, t:p.timestamp - zero/1e6, p }));
  let firstV = true, firstA = true;
  for (const x of mergeByTime(v, a)) {                                  // interleave: in-memory muxer writes chunks in add order (isobmff-muxer.ts:1649)
    if (x.k==='v') { await vs.add(new EncodedPacket(x.p.data, x.p.type, x.t, x.p.dur/1e6), firstV ? { decoderConfig: epochCfg(x.p.epoch) } : undefined); firstV=false; }
    else { await as.add(x.p.clone({ timestamp:x.t }), firstA ? { decoderConfig: aacCfgFixed() } : undefined); firstA=false; }
  }
  vs.close(); as.close(); await out.finalize();
  const poster = await makePoster(gops, posterAtUs ?? endUs - 1e6);    // VideoDecoder: decode from the GOP keyframe up to the target frame -> OffscreenCanvas 1280w -> convertToBlob('image/jpeg', 0.85)
  return { mp4: out.target.buffer, poster, durationSec: seconds };    // transferred to main -> new File([buf], `hanks-hits-${game}-${yyyymmdd-HHmm}.mp4`, { type:'video/mp4' })
}
```
- Timing measured on an M5: 12.8-19 ms for 30 s. Phone budget: 0.5 s or less for mux plus poster. This must be measured on the device matrix.
- Auto-highlights: on a brag event, wait for a 3 s post-roll, then build [event-12 s, event+3 s] in the background and keep it in memory or OPFS for the My Clips track.
- Filmstrip: while encoding, keep a 160x90 JPEG from each keyframe (clone the VideoFrame at 1 Hz, draw to a small OffscreenCanvas, convertToBlob). That is about 10 KB/s, so 600 KB per 60 s.
- Metadata: no location; creation time only. The title tag is the game name, never a name.

=== 6. EDITOR OPERATIONS (lazy editor chunk, run only while capture is paused) ===
6.1 Hardware-session rule: at most one hardware encoder at a time. Before any editor encode, flush and close the live encoder (and recreate it on resume as a new epoch; the avcC is normally identical). Some Android SoCs expose only 1-2 hardware sessions.
6.2 Trim:
- Start: exact at any frame, by negative pre-roll plus elst (proven).
- End: exact, by dropping trailing packets. This is valid because realtime mode has no reordering. If an epoch ever shows non-monotonic chunk timestamps, mark it hasReordering and use the render pass for end trims.
6.3 Title and end cards (smart-cut splice):
- Open ONE card encoder with epoch.encoderConfig (the identical object).
- Encode the title frames (IDR first).
- If the trim start is mid-GOP, decode that GOP with VideoDecoder and encode the frames from the trim point up to the next keyframe in the SAME card session.
- Compare the card session's avcC with the epoch's avcC. If identical, copy the ring packets from the next keyframe on, re-stamped after the title.
- Encode the end-card frames (IDR first) after the last gameplay timestamp.
- If the avcC differs, run the full render pass (6.4).
- Audio is always re-rendered from PcmRing: decode-free mixing of gameplay PCM with the jingle, encoded through AudioSampleSource with the priming shift. Every junction is sample-perfect, and this is cheap: 30 s took 100-160 ms natively.
6.4 Full render pass, used for format or orientation changes, avcC mismatches, share-size targets, or a mid-GOP start with a title card when the splice test fails. The source is the clip MP4: Input(BufferSource) goes through VideoSampleSink into the compositor (cards and overlays) and then VideoSampleSource({codec:'avc', latencyMode:'quality', keyFrameInterval:1, bitrate}). Audio re-renders from PCM. The target is 4-10x realtime on phones, with a progress UI. mediabunny has no smart cut: Conversion only copies at keyframes, with boundaryPolicy expand or shrink.
6.5 Share-size targets:
- Chromium Web Share cap is 50 MiB (share_service_impl.h). When a file is over 45 MiB, offer 'Make a share copy': bitrate = (45 MiB x 8 / duration) - 128 kbps, render pass. The download path is always available too.
- Hosted-link target (hosting track): 9.5 MB or less, same mechanism.

=== 7. FALLBACK MATRIX (final, device by device) ===
T1 (WebCodecs, native AAC):
- Chrome, Edge, Opera and Samsung Internet on Windows, macOS, ChromeOS and Android 94+. Linux desktop Chrome uses T1 video with WASM AAC.
- Chrome Android 150+ without a hardware H.264 encoder uses the OpenH264 software encoder (default since M150).
- Safari/iOS/iPadOS 26+ (native AAC with the 302253 fix).
T1 with WASM AAC:
- iOS/iPadOS 16.4-18.x (no AudioEncoder until 26). SIMD and nested workers are available from 16.4.
- Firefox desktop 130+ (no AAC encoder; avc through the platform/GMP, probe-encoded).
T2 (MediaRecorder H.264+AAC, hardware): iOS/iPadOS 15.4-16.3 and macOS Safari 14.1-16.3.
- One MediaRecorder per session on an HTMLCanvasElement compositor attached to the DOM (1x1 CSS px, opacity 0, pointer-events none, aria-hidden; WebKit bug 240380 made off-DOM canvases unreliable), plus captureStream(fps), plus the tap's MediaStreamAudioDestinationNode track.
- mimeType 'video/mp4', videoBitsPerSecond 2.5 Mbps (Safari's default is 10 Mbps), timeslice 1000.
- videoKeyFrameIntervalDuration 1000 is set where supported and ignored elsewhere.
- Blobs are posted to the worker. fmp4Segmenter keeps ftyp+moov plus a deque of moof+mdat fragments, reading each fragment's first-sample sync flag from trun/tfhd. The ring trims at keyframe-led fragments. Clips are built by mediabunny Input(BufferSource(init+fragments)) and EncodedPacketSink copies into a fast-start Output with the negative-timestamp trim and the 2112 priming shift (proven on Safari 26.5 output).
- Self-check at arm: if keyframe-led fragments arrive less often than every 5 s, switch to two staggered recorders (restart one every 30 s, lifetime 60 s). The older recorder then always covers at least 30 s, and the elst hides its extra pre-roll.
- On pause: stop and restart the recorder instead of calling MediaRecorder.pause(), which is buggy on iOS. Concatenate recorder segments only when their avcC match. Otherwise the clip covers the latest segment and the UI states its real length.
- Poster: the <video> element seeks and draws the frame (VideoDecoder does not exist before 16.4).
T3 (WASM H.264): Firefox Android, Chrome Android < 150 without hardware H.264, Firefox desktop < 130, and any WebAssembly browser where the T1 probe fails.
- minih264 (CC0) SIMD build in a dedicated worker, Constrained Baseline, 640x360 (480x270 on weak devices), adaptive 15-30 fps.
- Frames: VideoFrame, then copyTo RGBA (or ImageData where VideoFrame is missing), then SIMD RGBA-to-I420 in WASM, then encode. The GOP stays at 1 s.
- Wrapped as a mediabunny CustomVideoEncoder for offline renders and as a LiveVideo backend for the ring. The same ring and assembler apply.
- Audio: native AAC where it exists, otherwise our WASM AAC.
- Benchmark on M5 (2020 build, V8): 18 ms/frame at 360p. A phone needs the SIMD build; measure it on device.
T3-lite: iOS 14.3-15.3 (captureStream broken before 15.4 per WebKit 181663). Readback via drawImage then getImageData at 480x270 feeds the scalar minih264. Audio comes from the MediaRecorder('audio/mp4') fragment ring, remuxed.
NONE: brag card plus an explanation.
Rejected as final output: WebM VP8/VP9 (iOS Photos will not import it). Allowed only as a last-resort 'Android-only' file on a device that fails even T3-lite, labelled honestly.
Licensing note (T3 only, open question): self-distributed H.264 encoders carry no vendor patent license. The last US pool patents expire 2027-09-09; the Via LA free tier covers up to 100,000 units per year but requires signing.

=== 8. LONG 'RECORD' MODE (streams to OPFS, crash-safe) ===
- Recording tees the live packets: the same VideoEncoder output and the same AAC harness packets. There is no second encoder.
- Start: begin with the current GOP from the ring, giving up to 1 s of pre-roll that the elst trims to the exact tap time.
- Writer: in the worker, dir = navigator.storage.getDirectory() then 'clips/', and the file 'rec-<uuid>.partial.mp4' is opened with createSyncAccessHandle() (Safari 15.2+; createWritable is Safari 26-only and hit by mediabunny #459).
```ts
const sink = new WritableStream<StreamTargetChunk>({ write(c){ const d = (c.data.byteOffset===0 && c.data.byteLength===c.data.buffer.byteLength) ? c.data : c.data.slice(); h.write(d, { at: c.position }); } });
const rec = new Output({ format:new Mp4OutputFormat({ fastStart:'fragmented', minimumFragmentDuration:1, onMoof:(_,pos)=>{ pendingCommit = pos; } }), target:new StreamTarget(sink, { chunked:false }) });
// every 2 s: h.flush(); committed = pendingCommit (start of newest fragment = end of the previous complete one); write sidecar rec-<uuid>.json {committed, durationSec, avcCHex, startedAt}
```
- Stop: `await rec.finalize()`. Finalize to fast-start by reading Input(BlobSource(await fh.getFile())) through EncodedPacketSink per track into a new Output(Mp4OutputFormat({fastStart:'reserve'})) on a StreamTarget over a second sync handle, with maximumPacketCount set to the exact packet counts. This streams, with no whole-file RAM copy (proven: moov at byte 32). Then removeEntry the partial file and the sidecar.
- Crash recovery on the next load: for each .partial.mp4 with a sidecar, truncate(committed), finalize, and label the result 'Recovered recording'.
- Limits, justified by real constraints:
  - Storage: before start and every 10 s, estimate() must show at least 2.2 x the projected bytes (fMP4 plus final plus slack) free. The projection is about 23.3 MB/min at 3.1 Mbps. The derived limit is min(quota - usage - 500 MB, 30% of quota).
  - On a QuotaExceededError, stop gracefully and keep every committed fragment. The UI always says why it stopped.
  - A soft 'still recording?' prompt appears every 10 min. There is no hard cap.
  - Sharing: at most 50 MiB via Chromium share (6.5), with no documented iOS cap.
  - Call navigator.storage.persist() on first record. Tell the kid 'Save to Photos' is the durable copy, because WebKit evicts by LRU and after 7 days of no interaction.
- Sign-out must also wipe OPFS 'clips/' (hook into signOutAndClear; that belongs to the library track, but record.ts exposes wipeAll()).

=== 9. BUNDLE BUDGET AND NEXT.JS 16 / TURBOPACK ===
Budget (brotli):
- Main-thread shim: 12 KB or less, our code only. It is dynamically imported from ClipProvider after capability gating, and mediabunny never loads on the main thread.
- tap-worklet: about 1.5 KB.
- Engine worker initial: mediabunny live subset 38 KB plus our worker code of about 15 KB, 55 KB or less in total.
- Editor chunk (dynamic import inside the worker): about 45-58 KB incremental.
- AAC WASM: about 150-200 KB, only on devices without AAC.
- H.264 WASM: about 60-100 KB, T3 only.
- A typical iPhone 26 or Chrome visitor downloads about 70 KB total, and only after a game starts.
Next.js:
- REQUIRED: bump next and eslint-config-next from 16.2.9 to 16.3.x (16.3.6 latest). 16.2.x production builds break `new Worker(new URL(...))` (#94015, fixed by #96307 in v16.3.0), and next dev does not show it.
- Create the worker with `new Worker(new URL('./engine/engine.worker.ts', import.meta.url), { type:'module', name:'hh-clips' })`.
- Worker chunks are served under /_next/static and inherit the '/:path*' CSP ('unsafe-eval' covers WASM; worker-src 'self' blob: is fine).
- The worklet and WASM are versioned static files in public/clips, so no bundler wasm or worklet support is needed. They are served as application/wasm, with a fallback when instantiateStreaming fails.
- No CSP change is needed for local clips (media-src blob: is already present).
- Keep all of this out of the server bundle ('use client', dynamic ssr:false).
- If a Turbopack worker regression ever recurs: prebuild the worker with esbuild into public/clips/engine.<hash>.js in the build script (fallback plan, not the default).
- Pin mediabunny to exactly 1.60.0.

